WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.679
Abraham: Let's have a look at some
of the core terminology used in cyber

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:06.010
security, starting with Software.

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:09.610
Nicole: We all know that software
consists of instructions that

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:11.460
tell a computer system what to do.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:15.780
It is everything to do with the computer
that isn’t hardware, [the physical

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:21.330
components of a system.] Software can be
a set of programs compiled into a package

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:26.289
such as Internet browser, operating
system and general programs used to run

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:31.340
a computer, as well as complex systems
used for companies and organisations.

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:34.749
Without the software the
hardware wouldn’t run.

00:00:35.719 --> 00:00:39.300
Abraham: However, software can
also attack a computer and its

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.200
systems in the form of Malware.

00:00:41.769 --> 00:00:46.180
This is an umbrella term that describes
all forms of malicious software including

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:50.079
viruses, trojans, worms and ransomware.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.199
It can be used to steal information
or damage a system for malicious

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.599
reasons or just for financial gain.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:01.820
Anti-malware is key to keeping
systems secure against attack.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:07.830
Nicole: DoS and DDoS attacks
involve the ‘denial of service’

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.190
to the owner of the system.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:14.540
In a DoS attack a computer is used
to flood or crash a system with too

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:18.690
much internet traffic causing it
to slow down and eventually stop.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.090
This can be managed by using firewalls
at key points in a network [multiple

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.349
computers and devices connected together].

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:31.990
Firewalls are network security devices
that monitor, and filter incoming

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:36.550
and outgoing network traffic based on
previously established security policies.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.160
They are also as simple as a
barrier, that sits between a

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.020
home computer and the internet.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:46.820
Abraham: A DDoS ‘distributed denial
of service’ attack involves multiple

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:51.010
systems orchestrating a synchronised
attack by Botnets [interconnected

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:55.429
devices running software] directing
malicious network traffic or data to

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:57.679
a single target, overloading a server.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.850
These attacks can be seriously disruptive!

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:06.230
Nicole: Cloud technology allows access
to files and services from anywhere

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:07.760
in the world through the internet.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.380
It consists of a collection of
high-capacity storage computers

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:14.390
that remotely work as servers.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.699
Cloud Storage is very popular for the
safe storage of files due to its secure

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:23.600
accessibility anytime through Cloud
services, the infra- structure and

00:02:24.019 --> 00:02:26.510
software hosted by a third-party provider.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:31.850
Cloud services facilitate the flow of
data from the user via the internet

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:36.109
or VPN [virtual private network]
to their systems and back again.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.660
This can offer users, all the
facilities, without all the initial

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:44.530
financial outlay, for a fee depending
on how many services they use.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:49.810
Abraham: All data needs to be stored
and whether this is in the cloud, in

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:55.079
storage media and/or in transit it
needs to be ‘encrypted’ to be secure.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:00.390
Encryption is a form of cryptography
where a message is encoded so that

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:04.100
only those who should see it can
access it by means of a ‘key’.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:08.580
Encryption keys consist of
random strings of unintelligible

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:10.970
numbers, letters, and symbols.

00:03:11.429 --> 00:03:13.710
This is done to prevent theft by hackers.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:19.140
Nicole: BYOD stands for Bring Your
Own Device and involves employees

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.040
who use their own devices for work.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:25.119
With working from home and general
remote working becoming more common,

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.930
many individuals have their own
devices that they use for business,

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.120
as well as in a personal capacity.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:36.419
A BYOD policy sets any limitations
and restrictions on whether or not

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:40.560
a personal device can be connected
over the corporate network, depending

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.360
on the security systems in place.

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:45.730
More information on this
subject can be seen in our video

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:47.890
on BYOD and remote working.

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:49.140
Check out the link below.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:53.250
Abraham: We are always hearing about
hackers and how bad they are, but

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:55.230
sometimes hacking can be used for good.

00:03:55.850 --> 00:04:01.299
A PenTest or Penetration test is also
classed as ‘ethical hacking’ and is

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:05.790
an authorised simulated cyber attack,
on a computer system using ‘hackers

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:09.219
tools’, to assess whether there are
any exploitable vulnerabilities.

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:13.240
This is different to a ‘vulnerability
assessment’ which identifies,

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:17.000
quantifies, and prioritises
vulnerabilities in a system.

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:21.550
Nicole: Organisations often shift to
different domains for business and

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:26.190
technical reasons and may forget to
renew their older domains but still use

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:30.960
them in their code and infrastructure,
opening themselves up to Domain takeover.

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:35.290
The abandoned domains can offer
attackers an opportunity to take

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:36.930
advantage of this vulnerability.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:41.279
When an attacker registers the
abandoned domain, they own the domain

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:46.609
and its subdomains and other type
of DNS records and can exploit them.

00:04:47.389 --> 00:04:50.770
Pentesters can look for potential
domains that might exist and

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:52.340
be vulnerable to takeover.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:56.810
Subdomain takeovers generally
occur when a subdomain points to

00:04:56.810 --> 00:04:58.500
a service that no longer exists.

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:03.450
An attacker may take it over but
only that subdomain is affected.

00:05:03.989 --> 00:05:07.360
We can see through looking at
core terminology, that a Breach

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:11.250
in security can be made by a range
of unauthorised attacks, through a

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:16.100
variety of different means, and can
be on the whole much more damaging

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:17.910
than a physical breach of security.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:22.950
Cyber criminals have potential access to
everything and often it can be detected

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:24.939
too late to mitigate the effects.

